// voicechat: a spoken conversation with a local Qwen3, all on this machine's GPU. `run` is the whole program: it loads
// the three models, opens the microphone and the speaker, and loops.
//
//   listen            the microphone's samples go to voiceaudio's detector until it ends an utterance
//   -> Parakeet       speechtotext turns the utterance into text, which is printed ("you> ...")
//   -> Qwen3          llmrun answers in ChatML with thinking off, after a system line that it is speaking aloud
//   -> Kokoro         each sentence of the reply, the moment it is finished, goes to texttospeech while Qwen goes on
//                     writing the next
//   -> the speaker    voiceaudio's playback queue
//
// BARGE-IN. Speech heard while the reply is being written or played cuts the speaker's queue at once and stops the
// writing, and the loop listens. The model is then told only what you heard: the engine's cache is wound back to the end
// of the last sentence whose sound had begun to play, so what Qwen remembers of its answer is the sentences you began to
// hear (a sentence is the grain; no timing of words inside it).
// While sound plays the room can hear it, so the detector's thresholds are scaled up (`VoiceOptions.thresholdScale`,
// 3 by default; the reasoning is on the option). A headset avoids the problem.
//
//   voicechat.cone   this overview
//   session.cone     VoiceOptions, run: the models, the loop, the timings it prints
//   sentences.cone   Sentences (a reply cut into sentences as it arrives), speechText (a sentence cleaned for speech)
//   turns.cone       where a turn ends: Parakeet's punctuation decides whether a pause ends it (HeardTurn, isGoodbye, turnsIn)
//
// ENDING. Ctrl-C ends the loop at once (voiceaudio's Interrupt; SDL's audio otherwise swallows it), and so does a turn that
// is a goodbye ("Goodbye.", "Stop.": turns.cone says exactly which).
//
// NO SOUND UNLESS ASKED. The audio is named by `VoiceOptions.audio`: "disk" (microphone from a raw f32 file, speaker to
// one), "dummy" (silence in, nothing out) or "system" (the real microphone and speakers, the only one that makes sound).
//
// ONE THREAD. Qwen, Parakeet and Kokoro share the one gpu Device, which one thread uses at a time. The loop writes a
// token, and between tokens listens for speech and speaks a finished sentence (Kokoro runs on the same thread, 0.2 to
// 0.5 s for a sentence, during which no token is written and the microphone is not read, though SDL keeps what it
// hears). Since the reply is written several times faster than it is spoken, the only moment this costs is before the
// first sound; session.cone's header has the measured numbers.

mod voicechat;

import libc;
import stdio use *;
import collections use List, String;
import path use Path;
import time use Instant, Duration;
import thread;
import random use Rng;
import gpu use Device;
import llmrun;
import speechtotext;
import texttospeech;
import voiceaudio;
